  "details": "So let me start this out by saying that, while I love having an innate scaling system in the game, it should also be better than any alternatives we have had previously… Especially if we are now forced to use it with no other options! \n\nScaling has had multiple renditions even spanning back to the old days of 3.0 beta or before EAC was implemented into the game. Most of these old versions utilized bugs or workarounds to allow for scaling in some sort of shape or form, for better or for worse. These of course had their issues and such that came with them, but it is worth mentioning that, although they may have been more clunky, they still were much more accessible than the new version of scaling that was recently fully released! \n\nThe official scaling system is awesome in the sense that it allows for scaling without any need to use a mod or toggling OSC, but it also allows for much less overall by a long shot. There are a few things that I believe should be changed or taken into consideration if (hopefully more of when) an update to scaling occurs:\n\n-\tThe animation of scaling isn’t fluent.\n\nKind of a small one to start off with, but one thing is that when an avatar is scaled via effects of the world, like a flashlight that makes someone grow, there is a slightly janky rubber banding effect caused. I'm sure there is a reason behind the scenes as to why this occurs, but I felt like this is worth mentioning as non-official scaling systems could have a person grow over time without rubber banding of sorts. \n\n-\tChange the cap of scaling via the expression menu from 5 meters to 10 meters.\n\nAgain, not a huge thing, but 5 meters is just oddly restrictive. 10 meters feels like a relatively safe place to leave it due to the fact that scaling is on by default in worlds now. 10 meters would let people have a lot more fun with the system (like making my gorilla avatar the size of King Kong lol) while still not being obstructive to others. I also bring this up as, once again with alternate scaling systems or even just having your avatar bigger than 5 meters in unity, it's just an example of the official system doing less than alternatives which is imperative to keep in mind since those are no longer an option. \n\n-\tAllow for animations and parameters on avatars that can affect scaling. \n\nThis one is a big one as the scaling system has some pretty awesome potential if animations could be used on avatars that affect scaling. As an example, I’ve always wanted an animation where I spawn a mushroom from Mario that when eaten causes a sound as I become twice the size. Stuff like that is genuinely good fun and allows people to express themselves in way more ways than you might originally realize! In comparison to older alternatives, this was something possible before the implementation of EAC and is still to this day one of the main reasons people have been wanting scaling officially in the game. \n\nAdding onto this, although Udon can potentially allow for scaling effects, that means that an entire new scaling system is stuck utilizing its full potential to a select few worlds. When you put it into perspective like that, it’s a restrictive system that is even more so restricted to specific worlds. This is a big point to keep in mind due to this NOT being the case in its original implementation all the way back in avatar 3.0 beta.\n\n -      TLDR:\n\nWhat I am ultimately trying to explain in this thread is the fact that people wanted a scaling system implemented into the game innately for more accessibility than we already had, but as it stands that’s really not what we got. If players are going to be restricted to the new scaling system, it has to be better than what we have had previously, which as it stands, sadly isn’t the case.",
